#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167820} :activity/id #uuid "1dc924a5-3bab-5d8c-ba48-dfffbc669f9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050221 :topic/id #uuid "3748163a-70f7-518f-9382-77b8230a6624" :topic/math-academy-id 3237} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167977 :tutorial/content "Given two solutions to a linear system of differential equations, any *constant-coefficient* linear combination of those solutions is also a solution to the system. This property of linear systems is called the **linearity principle** (or the **superposition principle**).\n\nLet's state this again, more precisely. If $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to the homogeneous matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nthen the expression \n\n$$\nc \\, \\mathbf{u}(t) + d \\, \\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n \nis also a solution to that equation for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWe can prove this by substituting $\\mathbf x(t) = c\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ into the matrix differential equation and verifying that it results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ (c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t))^{′} & =A(c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t)) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t) & =cA\\,u(t)+dA\\,v(t) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t) & =c\\,{u}^{′}(t)+d\\,{v}^{′}(t)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c\\,\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\,\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is a solution!\n\nIn general, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations states that if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t), \\: \\ldots, \\: \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nare solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k \\, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7da5df73-af73-599d-a5cb-00e59aa241e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14181 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0bca23ee-5c63-5c99-a8e9-863925e0f706" :step/math-academy-id 586734 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166502 :question/id #uuid "e31ad4b2-40e2-5e36-95c0-7a961c53f302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21110"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412806}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "02afb27a-043d-58fd-8154-51ab6ac7e07a" :step/math-academy-id 586735 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166504 :question/id #uuid "ceb2d89c-fd0b-599f-a5ef-91e4be0d487a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393660}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"} :step/id #uuid "ca203f59-5153-5067-b096-48fda1d05ae1" :step/math-academy-id 586738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167978 :tutorial/content "If $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a *homogeneous* linear system \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an *inhomogeneous* linear system \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is some nonzero vector of functions, then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nThis gives us the **general solution** structure:\n\n$$\n\\text{(every solution)} = \\text{(homogeneous solution)} + \\text{(a particular solution)}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "89555a8b-f7ed-5bcf-90fc-f2a10d821828" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14182 :tutorial/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "c94e94e8-d20a-594d-88b3-fb65c0905501" :step/math-academy-id 586741 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166503 :question/id #uuid "f74662d2-1414-5716-9eee-2aba2ab7b0fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049851}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304814} {:db/id 